SAT_CHUNK("rw/inferences/2/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c65875fc84","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-2","passage":"<p>Many people assume that desert plants survive by storing large amounts of water. Some do, but a surprising number take a different approach. Certain desert shrubs drop nearly all of their leaves during dry months, reducing the surface through which water can escape. When rain returns, they grow a new set of leaves within days and resume making food. For these plants, survival depends less on saving water than on knowing when to stop using it.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, what would most likely happen to one of these shrubs if it kept all its leaves throughout the dry months?","choices":["It would grow faster during the dry months.","It would stop producing new leaves after the next rain.","It would store more water in its stems.","It would lose more water than it does when it drops its leaves."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Dropping leaves reduces \"the surface through which water can escape,\" so keeping them would mean losing more water. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6759b5ea98","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-3","passage":"<p>When the city opened its first public library branch in a converted bus station, officials expected it to be used mostly for borrowing books. Instead, the busiest part of the building turned out to be a row of tables with electrical outlets. Students came to charge laptops and do homework, job seekers used the free internet to fill out applications, and retirees gathered to read newspapers. Within a year, the library had added twice as many tables and cut back its shelf space.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the library most likely cut back its shelf space in order to","choices":["reduce the cost of buying new books.","make room for more work and gathering areas.","return the building to its original use.","store laptops for students."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It \"added twice as many tables and cut back its shelf space.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.302b539ed0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-4","passage":"<p>Researchers studying a population of lizards on a small island noticed that the lizards' toe pads, which help them grip surfaces, were larger than those of the same species on the mainland. The island's vegetation is dominated by smooth-barked trees, while the mainland population lives mostly among rough rocks. The researchers suggest that larger toe pads may give the island lizards a better grip on the slick bark they must climb.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which situation would the researchers most likely expect?","choices":["A population of the same species living on smooth-barked trees elsewhere would also have relatively large toe pads.","Lizards moved from the island to rough rocks would immediately lose their toe pads.","The island lizards would avoid climbing trees altogether.","Mainland lizards would climb smooth bark more easily than island lizards."],"answer":"A","explanation":"If smooth bark favors larger pads, other smooth-bark populations should show the same trait. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4c2c7d7ca4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-5","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The storm had taken the power at dusk, and by nine the house was silent except for the wind. Jalen's little sister had stopped crying an hour ago, mostly because he had turned the flashlight into a stage light and was performing every animal voice he knew. He ran out of animals around eleven. By then she was asleep, and he sat in the dark holding the flashlight, listening to the windows rattle and wishing someone would do the same for him.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, why did Jalen's sister most likely stop crying?","choices":["She was distracted and amused by Jalen's performance.","The storm had ended.","She had gone to sleep before the storm began.","The power had come back on."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The text says she stopped \"mostly because\" of his flashlight performance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.eea259c438","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-6","passage":"<p>For most of the twentieth century, many linguists regarded the \"uptalk\" pattern, in which statements end with a rising pitch, as a sign of uncertainty. More recent studies complicate this view. In recorded conversations, speakers who used rising pitch often did so while holding the floor, signaling that they had more to say, or while checking that listeners were following along. Rather than expressing doubt, the rise frequently seemed to invite engagement.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a speaker who uses rising pitch in the middle of a long story is most likely","choices":["admitting that parts of the story may be untrue.","trying to end the conversation.","asking a listener to tell a different story.","signaling that the story is not finished."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Speakers used rising pitch \"while holding the floor, signaling that they had more to say.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.39a9dd2a6f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-10","passage":"<p>Octopuses have a remarkable ability to taste with their arms. Each arm is lined with suckers containing thousands of chemical receptors, allowing an octopus to detect prey hidden in crevices simply by touching the surrounding rock. Researchers have also found that an arm can begin reacting to a promising scent before the octopus's central brain appears to direct it, suggesting that each arm processes some information on its own.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, an octopus exploring a rocky reef could most likely","choices":["taste only the prey that it can see.","detect a crab hidden inside a crack without seeing it.","avoid touching rocks while hunting.","locate prey only when its arms are still."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It can \"detect prey hidden in crevices simply by touching the surrounding rock.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0ce588a455","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-11","passage":"<p>When a small publisher decided to print its novels on paper made from recycled cotton clothing, some booksellers worried that readers would dislike the paper's slightly rough texture. The publisher included a short note at the back of each book explaining where the paper came from. Sales of the new editions matched those of the old ones, and many readers wrote to say that the note had made them notice and appreciate the feel of the pages.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the note at the back of each book most likely","choices":["caused sales of the new editions to fall.","replaced the need for a table of contents.","listed other books printed on recycled paper.","shaped how readers perceived the paper's texture."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Readers said the note \"had made them notice and appreciate the feel of the pages.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2a95f69ce6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-16","passage":"<p>Some city parks departments have begun replacing lawns with patches of native wildflowers and grasses. Lawns must be mowed, watered, and fertilized regularly, which is costly. Native plants, once established, need little water and almost no mowing. They also provide food for bees and butterflies. Critics argue that the plantings look untidy, but supporters point out that many residents have come to enjoy the changing colors through the seasons.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a parks department that switches from lawns to native plantings would most likely","choices":["spend less on mowing and watering once the plants are established.","attract fewer bees and butterflies.","receive no complaints about the parks' appearance.","need to fertilize its parks more often."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Native plants \"need little water and almost no mowing\" once established. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ab62478c4d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data1-18","passage":"<p>When engineers designed a new high-speed train, they faced an unexpected problem: each time the train exited a tunnel at full speed, it produced a loud boom that disturbed nearby residents. The cause was air compressed in front of the train's blunt nose. An engineer who was also a birdwatcher noticed that kingfishers dive from air into water, a much denser medium, with barely a splash. The team redesigned the train's nose to resemble a kingfisher's long, narrow beak, and the booms largely stopped.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the kingfisher's beak was a useful model most likely because it","choices":["helps the bird catch fish more quickly than other birds.","produces a loud sound when the bird dives.","allows the bird to move from one medium into a denser one with little disturbance.","is brightly colored and easy to see."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Kingfishers dive into denser water \"with barely a splash,\" similar to a train entering the dense air of a tunnel. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0114c388ef","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-30","passage":"<p>For centuries, people in some mountain villages have built terraces, flat steps carved into steep hillsides, to grow crops. Terraces slow the flow of rainwater so that it soaks into the soil instead of rushing downhill and carrying the soil away. In several regions where terraces were abandoned as families moved to cities, landslides and erosion increased within a few decades.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a village that maintains its terraces would most likely experience","choices":["more landslides than a village without terraces.","a decline in its crops.","less rainfall than neighboring villages.","less soil erosion than a similar village whose terraces were abandoned."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Terraces keep water from carrying soil downhill. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.59635f64e3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-33","passage":"<p>In the 1800s, many cities drew drinking water directly from rivers that also carried sewage. Outbreaks of cholera were common. When one city began filtering its water through layers of sand and gravel, cholera cases there dropped sharply, while a neighboring city that continued using unfiltered river water saw no such decline. The comparison helped persuade officials elsewhere to adopt filtration.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the neighboring city was useful to the comparison mainly because it","choices":["had no cases of cholera.","showed what happened when the water was not filtered.","had better water than the city that filtered.","invented the sand filtration method."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It continued using unfiltered water, serving as a point of comparison. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.19a3232a62","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data2-37","passage":"<p>A marine biologist studying cleaner wrasses, small fish that eat parasites off larger fish, noticed that the wrasses behaved differently when other fish were watching. When observed by potential clients, the wrasses were more careful to eat only parasites rather than nipping at the healthy mucus on their clients' skin, which they prefer. The biologist suggests that the wrasses may be managing their reputations.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a cleaner wrasse that is not being watched by other fish would most likely","choices":["clean more carefully than when it is watched.","refuse to eat any parasites.","leave the reef entirely.","be more likely to nip at a client's healthy mucus."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Wrasses prefer mucus and were more careful only when observed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.330fb0c02d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-39","passage":"<p>When the town of Millbrook lost its only grocery store, residents who did not own cars faced a forty-minute bus ride to buy fresh food. A group of retirees responded by organizing a weekly market in the church parking lot, inviting farmers from nearby counties to sell produce directly. The market began with four vendors. Five years later it hosts thirty, and the town council has voted to make the parking lot a permanent public market space.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the town council's vote most likely reflects","choices":["concern that the market has attracted too few vendors.","a decision to replace the market with a new grocery store.","recognition that the market has become an important and lasting resource for the town.","a plan to close the church."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The council made the lot a permanent market after the market grew from four vendors to thirty. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.cd4ac2dac2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-40","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every evening at six, Mr. Hadley walked the length of Harbor Road with his hands clasped behind his back, nodding to each neighbor by name. When he fell ill one winter and the walks stopped, no one mentioned it at first. But by the third evening, doors along Harbor Road began to open, and one neighbor after another found a reason to carry soup, or a newspaper, or simply a question, to the small blue house at the end of the street.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the neighbors most likely \"found a reason\" to visit Mr. Hadley because they","choices":["needed his help with a problem on Harbor Road.","were hoping to buy the small blue house.","wanted to check on him without making a fuss about his absence.","had been asked by a doctor to deliver his meals."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They bring small items or \"simply a question,\" suggesting a quiet way to check on him. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e59391d872","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-42","passage":"<p>Historians studying a seventeenth-century merchant's account books noticed that his profits rose sharply in the years after he began recording every transaction, however small, in a single ledger. Earlier, he had kept separate notebooks for each ship and often lost track of debts owed to him. The historians suggest that the change in record keeping, rather than any change in the goods he traded, may explain his improved fortunes.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which situation would most likely have contributed to the merchant's lower profits before the change?","choices":["A sudden drop in demand for all of the goods he sold.","A law requiring merchants to keep separate notebooks for each ship.","Money owed to him going uncollected because he could not track it.","A decision to stop trading overseas."],"answer":"C","explanation":"He \"often lost track of debts owed to him.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ef62e82ede","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-43","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The kettle hums before it sings, / a low and patient sound, / as if it knows the morning / needs a little time to come around. / And I, who rush from bed to door, / could stand to learn its art: / to warm up slowly, steadily, / before I make a start.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the speaker's usual morning routine is best described as","choices":["hurried.","carefully planned.","leisurely.","silent."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The speaker \"rush[es] from bed to door.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.dad8427045","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-47","passage":"<p>In the early twentieth century, a city built its main library with enormous windows on every side, at a time when most libraries relied on small windows and gas lamps. Critics complained that the glass would make the building cold and fade the books. The architect responded by placing reading tables near the windows and shelving the books in the building's interior, away from direct sunlight. The library's reading room became one of the most popular public spaces in the city.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the architect placed reading tables near the windows most likely to","choices":["make the building warmer in winter.","block sunlight from reaching the interior.","let readers take advantage of the natural light.","keep the books away from the tables."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Readers near windows benefit from light, while books stay in the interior. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1b8c1404f5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-51","passage":"<p>For centuries, residents of a desert region have built underground channels that carry water from distant mountain aquifers to their villages. Because the channels run beneath the surface, little water is lost to evaporation, even in extreme heat. Maintaining the channels requires workers to climb down vertical shafts to clear sand and repair walls, a skill traditionally passed from parents to children. As fewer young people learn the trade, some channels have fallen into disrepair.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the channels' underground design is most valuable in the region because","choices":["it allows the channels to be built without workers.","it makes the channels easy to see from a distance.","it prevents sand from ever entering the channels.","it protects the water from being lost in the region's intense heat."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Little water is lost to evaporation, even in extreme heat.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.341c8e74e7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-52","passage":"<p>When a zoo redesigned its gorilla habitat, keepers replaced the concrete floor with soil, logs, and tall grasses and scattered food throughout the space instead of placing it in a single pile. Within months, the gorillas spent far more time exploring and foraging and far less time sitting idle. The keepers also observed fewer conflicts among the animals, perhaps because food was no longer concentrated in one spot that dominant gorillas could control.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, conflicts among the gorillas were probably more common before the redesign because","choices":["dominant gorillas could control access to food placed in one pile.","the logs and grasses made the gorillas nervous.","the gorillas had too much space to explore.","the keepers scattered food throughout the habitat."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The keepers link fewer conflicts to food no longer being \"concentrated in one spot.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.cb96e692b3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-55","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Priya had practiced the violin piece for months, but in the recital hall her hands shook so badly that the first notes wavered. She thought of stopping. Then she noticed her grandfather in the third row, eyes closed, head tilted slightly, the way he listened to the radio on Sunday mornings. She drew the bow again, and this time the note held steady.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Priya's grandfather's posture most likely reminds her of","choices":["the radio program she dislikes.","a time when he was disappointed in her.","moments of relaxed, attentive listening that feel familiar and comforting.","the strict instructions of her violin teacher."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It echoes how he listened \"on Sunday mornings,\" a familiar scene. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.03b427ea69","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-56","passage":"<p>Many historians once believed that a certain medieval trade route declined because of a single event: the collapse of a major bridge. More recent work draws on tax records, merchant letters, and archaeological surveys of roadside inns. Together they suggest a gradual decline that began decades before the bridge fell, as new sea routes offered cheaper and faster ways to move goods. The bridge's collapse, in this view, hastened a change already under way.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which finding would best support the more recent view of the trade route?","choices":["Evidence that the bridge was rebuilt soon after it fell","Records showing that sea routes were rarely used in the period","A letter describing the day the bridge collapsed","Tax records showing that trade along the route was already falling well before the bridge collapsed"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The recent view holds that decline began decades before the collapse. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.06c0c9643f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-61","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My mother kept a garden that never looked like the ones in magazines. Tomato cages leaned at angles, the beans climbed a fence made from old bed frames, and the marigolds grew wherever their seeds happened to fall. Visitors sometimes offered to help her \"fix it up.\" She would smile, hand them a basket, and send them out to pick whatever was ripe.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the mother's response to offers to \"fix up\" the garden suggests that she","choices":["planned to hire a gardener.","wanted visitors to leave quickly.","did not see the garden's untidiness as a problem.","was embarrassed by the garden."],"answer":"C","explanation":"She smiles and invites them to harvest rather than tidy. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.370dfa1244","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-63","passage":"<p>For most of history, sailors navigated by the stars, but measuring longitude at sea remained extremely difficult. Longitude depends on knowing the time at a reference location while at sea, and ordinary clocks of the era lost accuracy on rolling ships. The problem was largely solved when clockmakers designed timekeepers precise and sturdy enough to keep accurate time through storms and temperature changes, allowing navigators to calculate their position far more reliably.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, before reliable sea clocks existed, sailors were most likely","choices":["forbidden from sailing in storms.","always certain of their exact location.","unable to see the stars at night.","often uncertain about how far east or west they had traveled."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Longitude measurement \"remained extremely difficult\" without accurate time. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e6b4d9ae7c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-64","passage":"<p>A school district tested a program in which high school students tutored younger children in reading twice a week. As expected, the younger children's reading scores improved. Less expected was the effect on the tutors: their own reading comprehension scores rose more than those of classmates who did not participate, and many reported feeling more confident speaking in class.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the tutors' improved comprehension most likely resulted in part from","choices":["receiving reading tutoring from younger children.","explaining and practicing reading skills while helping younger students.","taking extra reading classes instead of tutoring.","skipping their own classes."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Tutoring required them to work with reading material and explain it. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.78063e509c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data3-67","passage":"<p>Some languages have many more words for colors than others. For years, researchers debated whether speakers of such languages actually perceive colors differently. In one experiment, speakers of a language with separate words for light blue and dark blue were slightly faster at telling those two shades apart than speakers of a language with only one word for blue. The difference was small, but it suggests that the words people use may subtly influence how quickly they notice distinctions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers would most likely agree that","choices":["language may have a modest effect on color perception, but it does not determine what colors people can see.","speakers with one word for blue cannot see dark blue.","the experiment proved that language completely controls perception.","language has no connection to perception."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The effect was \"small\" and \"subtle.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.83c621d691","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-70","passage":"<p>Researchers wondered whether dogs understand where a person is pointing or simply follow the movement of a hand. In one test, a researcher pointed at one of two cups while standing still, then lowered her arm before the dog was released. Most dogs still went to the indicated cup, even though the arm was no longer moving or extended. The results suggest that dogs interpret pointing as a signal about a location, not merely as a motion to follow.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, lowering the arm before releasing the dog was important because it","choices":["removed the possibility that dogs were simply following a moving or extended arm.","made it easier for dogs to smell the food.","tested whether dogs could see the researcher.","encouraged dogs to ignore the cups."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It separated understanding from simply tracking motion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d8fc66de6c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-75","passage":"<p>A company that makes running shoes gave one group of runners shoes with thick cushioning and another group shoes with minimal cushioning. After six months, the two groups reported similar rates of injury. However, runners in each group tended to change how their feet struck the ground: those in minimal shoes landed more lightly on the middle of the foot, while those in cushioned shoes more often landed on the heel. The researchers suggested that runners may adapt their stride to their shoes.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a runner switching from cushioned to minimal shoes would most likely","choices":["land harder on the heel.","become injured more often.","begin landing more lightly on the middle of the foot.","stop running altogether."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Runners in minimal shoes landed \"more lightly on the middle of the foot.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c04ca5ac28","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-76","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>For weeks, Mei had avoided the old man who sold newspapers outside the station, embarrassed by her halting attempts at the language. One morning she was a coin short. Before she could apologize, he waved his hand, pressed the paper into hers, and pointed at a headline, slowly pronouncing each word. She repeated them after him. The next morning, she stopped at his stand on purpose.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Mei had avoided the newspaper seller mainly because she","choices":["disliked the newspapers he sold.","believed he charged too much.","felt self-conscious about her difficulty speaking the language.","was always late for her train."],"answer":"C","explanation":"She was \"embarrassed by her halting attempts at the language.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f4927c1400","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-82","passage":"<p>A researcher analyzing thousands of restaurant reviews noticed that reviewers used different words depending on price. In reviews of expensive restaurants, words associated with long, complex descriptions appeared more often, while reviews of inexpensive places more often compared food to comforting or addictive things. The researcher suggests that people may describe food partly to signal something about themselves, not only to describe the meal.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researcher would most likely agree that a restaurant review","choices":["is more accurate when the meal is expensive.","describes only the food and nothing else.","can reveal information about its writer as well as about the food.","is always written to criticize a restaurant."],"answer":"C","explanation":"People may describe food \"to signal something about themselves.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e26aacd27b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-83","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My grandfather's hands were maps of work, / the knuckles hills, the scars like rivers crossed. / He never told me where he'd been, / but I could read it there: / the rope burns of the fishing years, / the calluses from building walls, / the pale half-moon a saw once carved / the winter the barn was raised.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the \"pale half-moon\" is most likely","choices":["a birthmark the grandfather has had since childhood.","a scar from an injury the grandfather received while building a barn.","a tattoo of the moon.","a drawing on one of the grandfather's maps."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A saw \"carved\" it \"the winter the barn was raised.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.bc01298623","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-87","passage":"<p>For decades, a popular brand of breakfast cereal was sold in a tall box. When the company switched to a shorter, wider box containing the same amount of cereal, shoppers complained that they were getting less. Sales dipped until the company printed the weight in large numbers on the front and added a line stating \"Same amount, new shape.\" Sales then recovered.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, shoppers likely thought they were getting less cereal because they","choices":["noticed that the price had increased.","judged the amount by the box's height rather than its actual weight.","read the weight printed on the old box.","tasted a difference in the cereal."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The same amount in a shorter box seemed smaller. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.b13971b87d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-89","passage":"<p>Economists studying a coastal fishing town found that when the government set strict limits on how many fish each boat could catch, conflicts among fishers initially increased. Over time, however, fish populations recovered, catches became more predictable, and fishers' average incomes rose above their levels before the limits. Many fishers who had opposed the rules came to support them.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, many fishers most likely changed their view of the rules because","choices":["they saw the rules improve fish populations and their own incomes.","the government removed the rules.","conflicts among fishers increased over time.","they stopped fishing entirely."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Benefits over time led opponents to support the rules. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.127d0af258","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-90","passage":"<p>Some ancient Roman concrete structures, such as harbor walls, have survived in seawater for nearly two thousand years, while many modern concrete structures crack within decades. Researchers analyzing samples found that seawater seeping into the Roman concrete reacted with volcanic ash in the mixture, forming new minerals that filled cracks and made the material stronger over time.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, modern concrete intended for use in seawater might be improved by","choices":["making it thinner.","keeping seawater from ever touching it.","including ingredients that react with seawater to form strengthening minerals.","removing all volcanic ash from the mixture."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The Roman mixture's reaction with seawater made it stronger. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4e0e23b08e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-91","passage":"<p>When a city library began lending musical instruments, librarians expected mostly children to borrow them. The most frequent borrowers turned out to be adults who had played instruments decades earlier and wanted to see whether they could still play. Several formed a small band that now rehearses in a library meeting room every Thursday evening.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, many adult borrowers were most likely motivated by","choices":["curiosity about whether they still had skills they had once developed.","a requirement from their employers.","a desire to sell the instruments.","the library's rule limiting instruments to adults."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They \"wanted to see whether they could still play.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f1ef00ba91","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-93","passage":"<p>Scientists studying elephants noticed that the animals sometimes gathered and stood still, lifting a foot or leaning forward, even when no other elephants were visible. Recordings revealed that elephants produce very low-frequency rumbles that travel through the ground over long distances. Experiments suggested that elephants can detect these vibrations through sensitive nerve endings in their feet, which may explain the unusual postures.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, an elephant leaning forward with a foot lifted is most likely","choices":["preparing to charge at a predator.","paying attention to vibrations from distant elephants.","resting because it is tired.","searching for food on the ground."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The postures may relate to detecting ground vibrations. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.5b85df04b0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-94","passage":"<p>Many museums keep only a small fraction of their collections on display, storing the rest in warehouses. One natural history museum decided to let visitors view part of its storage area through glass walls, where scientists could be seen cataloging specimens. Visitor surveys showed that many people found the working storage area more interesting than some traditional exhibits, because it showed how museum research is actually done.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the museum's decision most likely helped visitors","choices":["handle specimens themselves.","view every item in the collection.","better understand what museum scientists do.","avoid traditional exhibits entirely."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It showed \"how museum research is actually done.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4380f1fc91","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data4-95","passage":"<p>Farmers in one region traditionally planted beans, corn, and squash together in the same field. Corn stalks give beans a structure to climb. Bean plants add nitrogen to the soil, which benefits the corn. Squash leaves spread across the ground, shading out weeds and helping the soil retain moisture. Each crop, in other words, helps the others grow.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if squash were removed from the field, the farmers would most likely face","choices":["more weeds and drier soil.","less nitrogen in the soil.","taller corn.","beans with nothing to climb."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Squash shades out weeds and helps the soil retain moisture. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.19048818ad","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-100","passage":"<p>Some sea turtles nest on beaches lined with hotels and streetlights. Hatchlings typically find the ocean by crawling toward the brightest horizon, which on a natural beach is the moonlit sea. Artificial lights can lure them inland instead, where many die. Towns that require beachfront buildings to dim or shield their lights during nesting season have seen far more hatchlings reach the water.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, hatchlings on a beach with bright lights behind it would most likely","choices":["reach the ocean more quickly.","wait until morning to move.","crawl toward the lights instead of the sea.","stay buried in the sand."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Artificial lights \"can lure them inland.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8bb4d142e4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-101","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>I learned to swim in a cold lake at the age of nine, taught by my older brother, who was eleven and far more confident than he had any right to be. His method was simple: he stood waist-deep and told me to swim to him. Each time I got close, he took one step back. I was furious. By the end of the summer, I could swim across the entire cove.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the brother stepped back each time the narrator got close most likely to","choices":["make her swim a little farther each time.","avoid being splashed.","keep himself from getting cold.","tease her until she gave up."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Stepping back extended her swim, building her ability. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d11b758885","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-103","passage":"<p>A company that designs video games tested two versions of a tutorial. In one, players read written instructions before starting. In the other, players learned controls gradually through short challenges during the first level. Players in the second group were more likely to finish the first level and to keep playing afterward. The company's designers concluded that people learn a game better by doing than by reading about it.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the designers would most likely recommend that a new game","choices":["begin with a long page of written rules.","remove its first level.","introduce its controls through hands-on challenges during play.","require players to read a manual before starting."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They concluded that people learn \"by doing.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2f2b77f2bc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-104","passage":"<p>Scientists once assumed that plants grow toward light simply because light speeds up growth. Later experiments revealed a more precise mechanism. A plant hormone gathers on the shaded side of a stem, causing cells there to lengthen faster than cells on the sunny side. Because the shaded side grows more, the stem bends toward the light.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if a plant's hormone gathered evenly on all sides of its stem, the stem would most likely","choices":["bend away from the light.","grow straight rather than bending toward the light.","bend toward the light more sharply.","stop growing entirely."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Bending results from uneven growth caused by the hormone's position. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1963eb8886","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-107","passage":"<p>Some cities have experimented with replacing traffic signals at busy intersections with roundabouts. At signals, cars sometimes speed through yellow lights, and the most dangerous crashes happen when vehicles collide at right angles. In roundabouts, all traffic moves in the same direction at lower speeds, so collisions that do occur tend to be glancing blows rather than direct hits. Studies of converted intersections have found large drops in serious injuries.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a crash in a roundabout is likely to be less severe than one at a signal because","choices":["traffic signals make cars move more slowly.","drivers in roundabouts never collide.","roundabouts have no cars.","vehicles are moving more slowly and in the same direction."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Collisions tend to be \"glancing blows rather than direct hits.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.00578a8aa7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-109","passage":"<p>A study of college students found that those who wrote down three things they were grateful for each week reported higher life satisfaction after ten weeks than students who wrote about daily hassles or neutral events. The effect was modest, and the researchers cautioned that the study relied on students' own reports of how they felt.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers would most likely agree that","choices":["writing about hassles is harmful.","students' self-reports are always inaccurate.","the study proves that gratitude journaling cures unhappiness.","further research using measures other than self-reports would be useful."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Their caution suggests a need for other kinds of evidence. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3b2de38c23","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-112","passage":"<p>Researchers tracking city foxes with GPS collars found that the animals adjusted their routines to avoid people. Foxes that lived near busy parks were active mainly after midnight, while foxes in quieter suburbs often moved about in the early evening. The researchers noted that the foxes' diets also differed, with park foxes eating more discarded human food.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, foxes living near busy parks were most likely active after midnight because","choices":["their GPS collars worked best at night.","fewer people were around at that time.","they preferred cold temperatures.","suburban foxes chased them away in the evening."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Foxes \"adjusted their routines to avoid people.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.32be90607c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-113","passage":"<p>A newspaper columnist argued that handwriting instruction wastes class time now that most writing is done on keyboards. A literacy researcher responded with evidence from studies of young children. Children who practiced forming letters by hand recognized letters more quickly and remembered spellings better than children who practiced only by typing. Handwriting, the researcher concluded, may support reading and spelling even if adults later write mostly by keyboard.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researcher would most likely respond to the columnist by noting that","choices":["handwriting instruction should begin in high school.","typing is unnecessary for adults.","children should never use keyboards.","the benefits of handwriting practice may extend beyond the ability to write by hand."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Handwriting \"may support reading and spelling.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c7491cd229","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-114","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The oak does not hurry. / It adds a ring a year, / a thin brown line of patience / no one sees. / Storms come, and summers, / and the children who climbed it / grow tall and move away, / and still it adds its ring.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"no one sees\" suggests that the oak's growth is","choices":["hidden underground.","too fast to observe.","gradual and easy to overlook.","visible only to children."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A thin ring each year is not noticed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.59c3253267","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-115","passage":"<p>Many people think of deserts as places with no water, but some desert animals obtain nearly all the moisture they need from food. Kangaroo rats, for example, rarely drink. They get water from seeds, and their kidneys produce extremely concentrated urine, conserving what little water they take in. They also spend the hottest hours in cool burrows, reducing moisture lost through breathing.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a kangaroo rat kept in a very hot enclosure with no burrow would most likely","choices":["lose more moisture than it would in the wild.","begin drinking seawater.","stop needing water entirely.","produce less concentrated urine."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Burrows reduce moisture loss during the hottest hours. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.56c61a2eae","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-116","passage":"<p>When a public radio station began publishing transcripts of its programs online, some staff feared that people would read instead of listen, reducing the audience. Traffic data showed the opposite. Many readers who found transcripts through internet searches clicked to listen to the original broadcasts, and the station's online listening hours increased.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the transcripts most likely helped the station by","choices":["preventing people from reading about the programs.","making its programs easier for new audiences to discover.","reducing the number of programs it produced.","replacing the need for broadcasts."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Readers found transcripts through searches and then listened. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.80fa955a8d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-119","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a nineteenth-century novel.</p><p>Mr. Ashworth prided himself on never changing his mind. He had decided at twenty that the new railway would ruin the countryside, and at seventy he still refused to ride it. When his granddaughter fell ill in the city, however, he was seen at the station before dawn, buying a ticket with shaking hands.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Mr. Ashworth's \"shaking hands\" most likely reflect","choices":["his excitement about riding a train for fun.","the cold weather in the countryside.","his anger at the ticket seller.","his worry about his granddaughter and the difficulty of breaking a long-held vow."],"answer":"D","explanation":"He is anxious about her illness and acting against his principles. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.55764250c9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data5-121","passage":"<p>Some schools have replaced traditional letter grades with detailed written feedback. Supporters argue that grades encourage students to focus on scores rather than learning. In one study, students who received only comments on their essays improved more on later assignments than students who received grades alone. Students who received both grades and comments, however, improved about as little as those who received grades alone, apparently paying attention mainly to the grade.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, students who received both grades and comments improved little most likely because","choices":["they focused on their grades and paid less attention to the comments.","they did not receive any feedback.","they wrote fewer essays.","their comments were less detailed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They were \"apparently paying attention mainly to the grade.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fbc2b8a86e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-126","passage":"<p>Scientists studying bamboo noticed that many species flower only once every several decades, and when they do, nearly all plants of the species flower at the same time, even in different countries. After flowering, the plants die. The simultaneous flowering produces an enormous quantity of seeds. Researchers suggest that this strategy may overwhelm seed-eating animals, which cannot eat all the seeds, allowing many to sprout.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if bamboo plants flowered at different times over many years, seed-eating animals would most likely","choices":["cause the bamboo to flower more often.","avoid eating bamboo seeds.","be able to eat a larger share of the seeds.","starve because no seeds were produced."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Staggered flowering would not overwhelm seed eaters. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.7c47a1629c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-128","passage":"<p>Some animals appear to understand basic numbers. In one experiment, researchers trained chicks that were only a few days old to find food behind a screen marked with a certain number of dots. When the number of dots stayed the same but the dots' size and spacing changed, the chicks still chose correctly most of the time. This suggests that the chicks were responding to quantity rather than to the overall appearance of the pattern.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if the chicks had chosen correctly only when the dots looked exactly the same as during training, researchers would most likely conclude that the chicks","choices":["understood numbers better than adults.","preferred one screen for unrelated reasons.","could not see the dots.","might have been responding to the pattern's appearance rather than quantity."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Success only with identical patterns would not show number sense. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6d758526ec","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-130","passage":"<p>When a regional power company offered customers a discount for using less electricity during late afternoon peak hours, officials expected only a small response. Many households began running dishwashers and washing machines later in the evening instead. Peak demand fell enough that the company postponed building a new power plant it had planned.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the company had planned to build a new power plant most likely because","choices":["customers asked for higher prices.","it wanted to shut down during the evening.","it expected peak demand to exceed its existing capacity.","the old plant produced too much electricity."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Lower peak demand removed the immediate need. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fc43aeb22c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-133","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Ana had always hated her middle name, Esperanza, which the other kids pronounced wrong on purpose. At her grandmother's funeral, the priest explained that Esperanza meant \"hope,\" and that her grandmother had chosen it during a hard winter when the family had very little. Walking out of the church, Ana said the name quietly to herself, trying it out as if for the first time.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Ana says the name \"as if for the first time\" because she","choices":["wanted to practice for a school play.","could not pronounce it correctly.","now sees it as meaningful rather than embarrassing.","had never heard the name before."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Its meaning gives the name new significance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e41f9a3056","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-135","passage":"<p>In the nineteenth century, many people believed that tomatoes, a member of the nightshade family, were poisonous. Some nightshade plants are indeed toxic, and tomato leaves contain small amounts of harmful compounds. The fruit itself, however, is safe to eat. As more people cooked with tomatoes and suffered no ill effects, the fear faded, and tomatoes became a staple in many cuisines.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, people's fear of tomatoes most likely faded because","choices":["tomato leaves became safe to eat.","the government banned other nightshades.","scientists removed toxins from all tomatoes.","experience showed that eating the fruit caused no harm."],"answer":"D","explanation":"People \"suffered no ill effects.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4d5a5cd22a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-137","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My mother's garden in December / is only sticks and string, / the tags still marking rows / of what the ground will someday bring. / She walks it anyway each morning, / hands deep in her coat, / reading the empty beds / like a letter no one wrote.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the tags in the garden most likely","choices":["indicate where plants will grow in the coming season.","show the prices of plants for sale.","label plants that died during the winter.","mark the graves of pets."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They mark \"what the ground will someday bring.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.02af3672d3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-138","passage":"<p>Scientists investigating why some people get more mosquito bites than others found that mosquitoes are attracted partly by carbon dioxide and certain skin chemicals. People who produced more of particular fatty acids on their skin attracted mosquitoes far more strongly in lab tests, and this preference remained consistent when the same people were tested months apart.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a person who produces unusually high amounts of the relevant fatty acids would most likely","choices":["never be bitten by mosquitoes.","repel mosquitoes from nearby people.","tend to be bitten more often than others.","attract mosquitoes only in winter."],"answer":"C","explanation":"These people \"attracted mosquitoes far more strongly.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.45597f1cca","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-146","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The new teacher did not take attendance by reading names. Instead, on the first morning, she asked each student to say one thing they had noticed on the way to school. The answers were slow at first: a red car, a barking dog. By Friday, students were describing the shape of clouds, the smell of rain on hot pavement, and the exact number of steps from the bus stop to the door.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the teacher's routine was most likely intended to","choices":["shorten the school day.","test students' memory of names.","encourage students to pay closer attention to the world around them.","find out which students rode the bus."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Students become more observant over the week. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.47463fa058","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data6-147","passage":"<p>Engineers designing a new stadium roof studied the structure of bird bones, which are hollow but reinforced with thin internal struts. The design allows bones to be light yet strong. Using a similar approach, the engineers created roof beams with hollow interiors crossed by thin supports, reducing the roof's weight by nearly a third without sacrificing strength.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the thin internal supports in the roof beams most likely serve to","choices":["let light into the stadium.","make the beams heavier.","add strength while keeping the beams light.","attract birds to the roof."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Like struts in bones, they reinforce hollow beams. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.7ff6aaf674","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-150","passage":"<p>When a university cafeteria stopped offering trays, officials hoped to save water used for washing them. They also found that food waste dropped by about a quarter. Without trays, students carried fewer dishes at once and tended to take only what they planned to eat, returning for seconds if they were still hungry.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, students without trays wasted less food most likely because","choices":["carrying fewer dishes led them to take smaller amounts at once.","fewer students ate in the cafeteria.","the cafeteria served smaller portions.","students were fined for leaving food."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They \"tended to take only what they planned to eat.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0c7c67e001","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-152","passage":"<p>Researchers studying sleep in dolphins found that the animals rest one half of their brain at a time while the other half stays alert. During this state, a dolphin keeps one eye open, surfaces regularly to breathe, and continues to watch for threats. After a period of time, the halves switch roles.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, this form of sleep is most likely useful to dolphins because","choices":["it allows them to dream more.","they need to hunt continuously.","they must keep breathing at the surface and stay alert even while resting.","it prevents them from ever being tired."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They \"surface regularly to breathe\" and watch for threats. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.41f21c1aca","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-153","passage":"<p>A city that installed sensors to monitor noise levels discovered that the loudest hours in one neighborhood were not late at night, as residents' complaints had suggested, but early in the morning, when delivery trucks unloaded at restaurants. Officials worked with businesses to shift deliveries to midday, and noise complaints fell.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, residents' complaints before the sensors were installed were most likely","choices":["caused by sensor malfunctions.","entirely invented.","inaccurate about when the noise was worst.","focused on midday deliveries."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Complaints suggested late night, but the loudest hours were early morning. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.4280f24c11","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-154","passage":"<p>For years, scientists debated whether some dinosaurs cared for their young. Discoveries of fossilized nests changed the conversation. At one site, adult skeletons were found sitting on clutches of eggs in positions resembling those of brooding birds, suggesting that the adults were protecting or warming the eggs when they died.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the adults' positions were significant because they","choices":["indicated that the adults were running away.","proved that the eggs never hatched.","showed that the adults were eating the eggs.","resembled those of modern birds that care for eggs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They resemble \"brooding birds.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8bd81baeee","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-155","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay.</p><p>The first time I saw the ocean, I was sixteen and disappointed. I had imagined something like the postcards: impossible blue, gentle waves. The real ocean that day was gray and loud and cold, and it smelled of salt and seaweed. It took me years to understand that I had not been disappointed by the ocean. I had been disappointed by the postcards.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the details about the gray, loud, cold ocean mainly emphasize","choices":["the pollution of the beach.","the contrast between reality and the writer's expectations.","the danger of swimming in the ocean.","the writer's love of cold weather."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They contrast with the \"impossible blue\" postcards. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.a7a1f04c57","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-156","passage":"<p>When a school introduced a program in which students grew vegetables in a garden and then cooked them in class, researchers surveyed students' eating habits before and after. Students in the program reported eating more vegetables at home, and many said they were more willing to try unfamiliar foods. Students at a similar school without the program showed no such change.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, growing vegetables themselves most likely made students","choices":["more interested in eating and trying foods.","uninterested in gardening.","less willing to eat vegetables.","unwilling to cook at home."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Students became more willing to try unfamiliar foods. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.da299368b3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-159","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The lighthouse keeper had not spoken to another person in three weeks when the supply boat arrived. He had rehearsed what he would say: news about the storms, a request for more lamp oil. But when the young sailor jumped onto the dock and said hello, the keeper found that all he wanted was to hear the boy talk, about anything at all.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the keeper's reaction most likely reflects","choices":["his worry about the storms.","his lack of interest in supplies.","his dislike of the young sailor.","the loneliness of his long isolation."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Three weeks without speaking to anyone. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c9f29e5023","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-160","passage":"<p>A tech company tested two versions of a sign-up page. One asked visitors for their name, email, phone number, and address. The other asked only for an email address. The shorter form had nearly twice as many sign-ups. The company concluded that each additional request for information discourages some visitors from completing the process.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a company that wanted more newsletter sign-ups would most likely benefit from","choices":["adding more required questions.","hiding the sign-up form.","requiring visitors to create passwords first.","requesting only the information it truly needs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Each additional request discourages visitors. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.856a06d895","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-162","passage":"<p>When a museum added benches to its galleries, curators noticed that visitors spent more time looking at individual artworks. Before, most people walked past paintings quickly while standing. With a place to sit, visitors often stayed in front of a single piece for several minutes, and some reported noticing details they would otherwise have missed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, visitors noticed more details most likely because they","choices":["could comfortably look at a work for a longer time.","were required to take notes.","were given magnifying glasses.","visited fewer galleries."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Seating allowed them to stay several minutes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2607e4e0c0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-165","passage":"<p>Engineers designing bridges in earthquake zones once focused on making structures as rigid as possible. Newer designs often include flexible bearings and dampers that allow parts of a bridge to move slightly during shaking. By absorbing energy rather than resisting it, these features can reduce damage to the main structure.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a completely rigid bridge would most likely","choices":["move easily during shaking.","have to resist earthquake forces rather than absorb them.","be immune to earthquake damage.","require dampers to remain rigid."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Rigidity resists rather than absorbs energy. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2cda170e81","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-167","passage":"<p>For decades, a small town's annual fair featured a pie contest judged by the same three residents. When the organizers began inviting judges from neighboring towns, several longtime participants worried that outsiders would not appreciate local recipes. Instead, entries increased, and a pie made with a rarely used local fruit won first prize for the first time, drawing new attention to the fruit.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the winning pie most likely drew attention to the local fruit because","choices":["the judges were growers of the fruit.","the fruit was banned from the contest.","few people had used the fruit in contest entries before.","the fruit was imported from another country."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It was a \"rarely used local fruit.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2beecfcec5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-168","passage":"<p>Scientists studying why zebras have stripes tested several ideas. When horses wore striped coats, biting flies landed on them far less often than on horses wearing solid-colored coats, although flies still approached. The researchers suggest that stripes may confuse flies as they try to land, which could help zebras avoid insect-borne diseases.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, zebras' stripes would be most beneficial in regions where","choices":["zebras live in cold climates.","flies are absent.","predators hunt at night.","biting flies spread diseases."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Stripes may help avoid \"insect-borne diseases.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e18034dae8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-169","passage":"<p>A city experimented with \"complete streets,\" redesigning several roads to include wider sidewalks, bike lanes, and crosswalks with shorter crossing distances. Some drivers predicted longer commutes. Travel times for cars increased only slightly, while the number of pedestrians and cyclists on those streets rose substantially, and serious crashes declined.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, city officials would most likely view the redesign as","choices":["a failure that should be reversed.","irrelevant to pedestrians.","largely successful, given its safety and mobility benefits.","harmful to cyclists."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Benefits outweighed a small increase in travel time. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8cae878c6c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-172","passage":"<p>Many desert plants have waxy coatings on their leaves. The wax reduces water loss by sealing the leaf surface. Some plants in rain forests also have waxy leaves, but for a different reason: the slick surface helps rainwater run off quickly, preventing mold and algae from growing on the leaves.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a rain forest leaf without a waxy coating would most likely","choices":["shed rain more quickly.","stay wet longer and be more likely to grow mold or algae.","lose water faster in dry air.","be immune to mold."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Wax helps water run off, preventing growth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.17340c4a36","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-173","passage":"<p>When a regional newspaper began publishing weekly articles written by high school students about their communities, editors expected little interest from adult readers. Instead, the student articles were among the most read on the paper's website, and several local officials cited them at public meetings as helpful views of issues they had overlooked.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, local officials valued the student articles most likely because they","choices":["offered perspectives on issues the officials had not fully considered.","were written by professional journalists.","agreed with every decision officials made.","were published only in print."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They were \"helpful views of issues they had overlooked.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.80ba955665","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-174","passage":"<p>Scientists once thought that adult brains could not produce new neurons. Studies of songbirds, and later of mammals, found evidence that new neurons form in certain brain regions throughout life. In some animals, the formation of new neurons increased with learning and exercise, though researchers continue to debate how much this occurs in adult humans.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, scientists would most likely want further research to determine","choices":["whether songbirds can sing.","whether the brain has neurons at all.","how much new neuron formation occurs in adult humans.","whether exercise exists."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The human question \"continue[s] to be debated.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f24bc98e44","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data7-175","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Marco had lived above the bakery for ten years and had long since stopped noticing the smell of bread. Then he spent a month traveling for work. On the night he returned, he climbed the stairs, opened his door, and stopped, overwhelmed by the warm scent of yeast and butter drifting up through the floorboards, as though the building itself were welcoming him home.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Marco notices the smell on his return most likely because","choices":["the bakery had started baking a new kind of bread.","being away made the familiar scent new to him again.","he had never smelled bread before.","the floorboards had been removed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A month away renewed his awareness. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.708fd9e260","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-177","passage":"<p>When a city began offering free swimming lessons to children in neighborhoods without public pools, officials bused students to a recreation center across town. Participation was lower than expected. Surveys found that many families could not accommodate the long bus rides after school. The city then set up temporary pools at neighborhood schools during the summer, and enrollment tripled.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the main barrier to participation was most likely","choices":["a lack of interest in learning to swim.","the cost of the lessons.","poor teaching at the recreation center.","the time and difficulty of traveling to the lessons."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Families \"could not accommodate the long bus rides.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.548b9154de","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-179","passage":"<p>Researchers studying how people navigate cities found that residents who relied heavily on GPS directions formed less accurate mental maps of their neighborhoods than residents who navigated without it. When asked to draw maps or estimate distances between landmarks, GPS users made more errors, though they reached destinations just as reliably.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a GPS user whose phone battery died in an unfamiliar part of town might","choices":["draw a perfect map of the area.","never have used GPS before.","have more difficulty finding the way than someone who usually navigates without GPS.","find the way more easily than other residents."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Weaker mental maps would make navigation harder without GPS. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.6f1f9862cf","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-180","passage":"<p>For centuries, scholars assumed that a certain carved stone monument was a religious object. Recent analysis of the carvings revealed markings that align with the positions of the sun at the solstices and equinoxes. Some researchers now propose that the monument may also have served as a calendar, helping communities plan planting and festivals.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the word \"also\" in the final sentence suggests that the researchers","choices":["do not necessarily reject the monument's religious significance.","believe the monument had no religious purpose.","doubt that the markings align with the sun.","think the monument was built recently."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Also served\" adds a function. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.785d245ae6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-181","passage":"<p>When a hospital began having pharmacists review patients' medication lists at discharge, errors in prescriptions sent home with patients dropped sharply. Pharmacists often caught medications that duplicated one another or could interact harmfully, problems that busy doctors had missed. Readmissions related to medication problems also declined.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the decline in readmissions most likely resulted from","choices":["the hospital refusing readmissions.","patients staying in the hospital longer.","doctors prescribing more medications.","fewer patients going home with problematic medication combinations."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fewer errors meant fewer medication-related problems. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f5a4998cba","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-183","passage":"<p>Ecologists found that frogs in ponds near busy roads call at higher pitches than frogs of the same species in quiet ponds. Traffic noise is concentrated at low frequencies, and higher-pitched calls may be easier for other frogs to hear over that noise. However, females of the species generally prefer lower-pitched calls, which typically come from larger males.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, male frogs near busy roads may face a trade-off between","choices":["finding food and avoiding predators.","living in ponds and living on land.","calling at night and calling during the day.","being heard over traffic and sounding attractive to females."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Higher pitch helps audibility but lowers appeal. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.15dd1a9893","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-185","passage":"<p>When scientists first proposed that the continents slowly move, many geologists objected because no one could explain what force could push such enormous masses. Decades later, maps of the ocean floor revealed long underwater mountain ranges where new crust forms and spreads outward. This discovery provided a mechanism, and the idea of moving continents gained broad acceptance.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, geologists initially rejected the idea mainly because","choices":["moving continents would be too fast to observe.","the idea was proposed by ocean explorers.","maps showed continents in fixed positions.","it lacked an explanation of how continents could move."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"No one could explain what force could push\" them. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.154b11547f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-187","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Kenji's father spoke little English and rarely came to school events. So when Kenji saw him standing at the back of the auditorium during the science fair, still in his work uniform, Kenji nearly dropped his poster. His father did not ask questions or read the display. He simply stood beside it for the entire evening, nodding at every visitor who stopped.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the father's \"still in his work uniform\" suggests that he","choices":["planned to work at the science fair.","did not own other clothes.","came directly from work to be there.","was a judge at the fair."],"answer":"C","explanation":"He arrived without changing, likely from work. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.66447d2c65","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-188","passage":"<p>Biologists studying hibernating ground squirrels found that the animals periodically warm themselves up during hibernation, raising their body temperature for about a day before cooling down again. These arousals use a large share of the energy the squirrels spend over the winter. Researchers suspect the arousals may be necessary for functions such as sleep or immune activity that cannot occur at very low body temperatures.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, arousals would be unlikely to continue if they","choices":["provided no benefit to the squirrels.","used a large amount of energy but served essential functions.","happened during winter.","occurred every few weeks."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Researchers assume arousals serve necessary functions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.945fc78072","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-189","passage":"<p>A museum that offered free admission one evening a month found that the free nights attracted visitors who had never been to the museum before, including many families with young children. Membership sign-ups also rose after these evenings, suggesting that some first-time visitors returned as paying members.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the museum most likely viewed the free evenings as","choices":["a reason to raise ticket prices for families.","a loss with no benefits.","an event only for existing members.","a way to attract new audiences who might become long-term supporters."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They attracted first-time visitors and new members. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.bcb0427882","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-190","passage":"<p>Scientists examining ice cores from Greenland found layers of dust and chemicals that correspond to large volcanic eruptions. By counting annual layers of snow, much like tree rings, researchers could date the eruptions and compare them with historical records of cold summers and poor harvests. Several recorded famines coincided with volcanic layers in the ice.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the link between volcanic layers and famines suggests that eruptions may","choices":["have no connection to weather.","have occurred only in Greenland.","have caused warmer summers.","have cooled climates enough to harm harvests."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Famines coincided with volcanic layers and cold summers. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ef905b325b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-191","passage":"<p>When a small town's only doctor retired, residents feared they would lose local medical care. The town partnered with a nearby hospital to set up video appointments at the library, staffed by a nurse who could take measurements during calls. Many residents were initially skeptical, but within a year most routine visits happened this way, and patients reported saving hours of travel.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the nurse's presence during calls was likely important because it","choices":["allowed measurements that a remote doctor could not take alone.","replaced the need for a doctor entirely.","kept the library quiet.","taught residents to use computers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The nurse \"could take measurements during calls.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0ec2237615","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-192","passage":"<p>Researchers analyzing thousands of job postings found that ads using words such as \"competitive\" and \"dominant\" attracted fewer applications from women than ads describing the same jobs with words such as \"collaborative\" and \"supportive.\" When a company revised its ads to use more neutral language, the share of women applying increased.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a company hoping to attract a wide range of applicants would most likely benefit from","choices":["using more words like \"dominant.\"","removing all job descriptions.","reviewing the language used in its job ads.","posting fewer job ads."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Wording influenced applicant pools. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ac0e6b62d0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data8-193","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The river does not remember / the mountain it came from, / nor does it know the ocean / where it will someday run. / It only knows the stones it turns / and the reeds along its way, / and it sings to them the one song / it has: today, today.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the river's \"one song\" most likely represents","choices":["fears about the future.","the sound of the ocean.","attention to the present moment.","memories of the mountain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Today, today.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.617840d40f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-198","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The letter from her brother arrived on the first day of spring, three years after their last argument. Mira read the single line twice—\"I planted the tomatoes the way you always said\"—and then sat down on the porch step and laughed until she cried. Their fight had been about something far larger than tomatoes, but she understood exactly what he meant.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the brother's line most likely suggests that he","choices":["needs help with his garden.","is planning to visit the next day.","is acknowledging Mira and wants to repair their relationship.","is still angry about the argument."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Doing it \"the way you always said\" concedes to her. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d1d527f030","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-200","passage":"<p>In the early twentieth century, many cities replaced horse-drawn carriages with automobiles, partly to solve a pollution problem: horse manure filled streets, attracting flies and spreading disease. Cars eliminated that problem but introduced new ones, including exhaust fumes and traffic accidents, that later generations would struggle to address.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the author would most likely agree that","choices":["solutions to problems can create unexpected new challenges.","traffic accidents were common with horses.","exhaust fumes were a problem before cars.","horses were better than cars in every way."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cars solved one problem while creating others. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3be21f629e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-201","passage":"<p>A psychologist studying how people judge the passage of time asked participants to estimate how long they had spent on various tasks. People consistently felt that time passed faster when they were absorbed in engaging activities and slower when they were bored or waiting, even when the actual durations were identical.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, someone waiting for a delayed train with nothing to do would most likely","choices":["feel that the wait lasted longer than it did.","estimate the wait perfectly.","enjoy the wait more than an engaging activity.","feel that the wait was very short."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Waiting makes time feel slower. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8f0bf8b6e7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-202","passage":"<p>When scientists studied honey found in ancient tombs, they discovered that some of it was still edible after thousands of years. Honey's low water content and high acidity make it inhospitable to bacteria, and bees add an enzyme that produces small amounts of hydrogen peroxide, which further prevents spoilage.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, honey that absorbed a large amount of water would most likely","choices":["turn into hydrogen peroxide.","become more acidic.","become more vulnerable to spoilage.","last even longer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Low water content helps prevent bacteria. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.83c361ec5d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-203","passage":"<p>A city that offered residents free compost bins found that participation varied widely by neighborhood. In areas where volunteers went door to door explaining how composting works, many more households used the bins than in areas where bins were simply delivered with printed instructions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a city launching a new recycling program would most likely benefit from","choices":["limiting the program to one neighborhood.","relying only on printed flyers.","having people explain the program directly to residents.","delivering bins without any instructions."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Personal outreach increased use. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.0f7d044a52","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-204","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My first apartment had a window that looked directly at a brick wall. For a year I resented it. Then one spring a pair of sparrows built a nest in a gap between the bricks, and I found myself waking early to watch them carry grass and string. By the time the chicks fledged, I had stopped thinking of it as a bad view.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the narrator's waking early suggests that she","choices":["wanted to chase the birds away.","became interested in and attached to the sparrows.","needed to leave for work earlier.","had trouble sleeping because of noise."],"answer":"B","explanation":"She watched them carry nesting material. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.42716c2229","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-210","passage":"<p>When a company introduced a policy allowing employees to work any hours they chose as long as they completed their tasks, managers worried that collaboration would suffer. Communication did become harder at first. Teams responded by agreeing on a few core hours when everyone would be available, and surveys later showed high satisfaction with the flexible schedule.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, core hours were most likely intended to","choices":["replace the flexible schedule entirely.","make sure employees could communicate with one another at predictable times.","reduce employee satisfaction.","require everyone to work longer days."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"When everyone would be available.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ba67300e79","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-211","passage":"<p>Biologists noticed that some species of butterfly that are harmless to birds closely resemble species that taste bitter or are toxic. Birds that have learned to avoid the toxic butterflies also tend to avoid the harmless look-alikes. The resemblance, researchers suggest, protects the harmless species even though it has no defenses of its own.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if toxic butterflies disappeared from a region, the harmless look-alikes would most likely","choices":["become toxic themselves.","be avoided even more strongly.","lose some of the protection their appearance provides.","change color immediately."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Birds would no longer learn to avoid the pattern. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fa1e8f12e3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-212","passage":"<p>Historians studying diaries from an eighteenth-century city found that residents frequently mentioned the price of bread. Entries describing price increases often appeared shortly before records of public protests. Some historians argue that the cost of basic food played a larger role in the city's unrest than political speeches, which receive more attention in traditional accounts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, traditional accounts of the city's unrest most likely","choices":["ignored political speeches entirely.","described bread prices in detail.","relied mainly on residents' diaries.","focused more on political speeches than on food prices."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Speeches \"receive more attention in traditional accounts.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d2eb40642f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data9-214","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>I kept my grandmother's thimble / in a drawer for twenty years, / a silver cap too small / for any finger but her own. / Tonight I sewed a button / on my daughter's winter coat, / and without thinking, / I slipped the thimble on.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"without thinking\" suggests that the speaker's action was","choices":["required by her daughter.","a deliberate decision made after long reflection.","a mistake she regretted.","instinctive and connected to her grandmother's influence."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It happens naturally, echoing the grandmother. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8447c5d4fc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-218","passage":"<p>Researchers studying rats found that animals given access to running wheels learned their way through mazes faster than rats without wheels. The exercising rats also showed more new neuron growth in a brain region associated with memory. The researchers caution that rats differ from humans but suggest that exercise may support learning in other animals as well.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers would most likely want to test next whether","choices":["similar effects of exercise occur in other species.","mazes are harder without walls.","rats prefer wheels to food.","rats can run faster than mice."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They suggest exercise \"may support learning in other animals.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.cfb4227615","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-226","passage":"<p>Engineers testing a new type of road surface made of porous pavement found that rainwater drained through it rather than running off. During storms, streets with the new surface had less standing water, and nearby storm drains overflowed less often. In cold regions, however, water trapped in the pavement could freeze and damage it, requiring more maintenance.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, porous pavement would most likely be most practical in","choices":["regions with long, freezing winters.","underground tunnels.","areas with no rainfall.","regions with frequent rain and mild winters."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It helps with storms but struggles with freezing. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d3dfe742a7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-228","passage":"<p>For many years, cities built public housing as large, isolated high-rise complexes. Critics later argued that concentrating many low-income families in one place limited access to jobs and services. More recent programs have favored mixed-income developments spread throughout neighborhoods, which some studies suggest can improve residents' access to opportunities.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, supporters of mixed-income developments most likely believe that","choices":["families should be concentrated in one area.","spreading housing throughout neighborhoods gives residents better access to jobs and services.","housing has no effect on opportunity.","high-rise buildings are always unsafe."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They respond to criticism about limited access. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d0b3aeba04","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data10-230","passage":"<p>Botanists studying seeds stored in a frozen seed bank found that most seeds remained capable of sprouting after decades, but the rate varied widely by species. Some seeds lost viability quickly even when frozen, suggesting that seed banks may need different storage methods, or more frequent replanting and collection, for certain plants.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a seed bank storing seeds of a species that loses viability quickly would most likely need to","choices":["plant all its other seeds immediately.","freeze the seeds at warmer temperatures.","stop collecting that species.","replace its stored seeds more often or find another storage method."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Different methods or more frequent replanting. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3c25f50035","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-232","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The piano had been out of tune for years, and no one in the house played it anymore. When Aiko's grandmother moved in, she said nothing about it for a week. Then one evening, after everyone had gone to bed, the family heard music drifting up the stairs: an old, slightly sour-sounding waltz, played slowly, as if the player were remembering it one note at a time.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the phrase \"as if the player were remembering it one note at a time\" suggests that the grandmother","choices":["had not played the piece in a long time.","wanted to wake the family.","was reading unfamiliar sheet music.","was an expert concert pianist."],"answer":"A","explanation":"She recalls the waltz slowly. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c4153174d5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-233","passage":"<p>Researchers studying why some people are \"morning people\" and others \"night owls\" found that genetic differences affect the body's internal clock. However, when volunteers spent a week camping without artificial light, their sleep schedules shifted earlier and became more similar to one another, suggesting that modern lighting widens the natural differences between people.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a night owl who spent a week without artificial light would most likely","choices":["begin going to sleep earlier than usual.","become a morning person permanently.","stop sleeping altogether.","stay awake even later."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Schedules \"shifted earlier.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.b824a5604e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-235","passage":"<p>Some desert beetles collect drinking water from fog. The beetles stand on sand dunes with their backs tilted into the wind, and tiny droplets of fog collect on bumps on their shells, eventually rolling down into their mouths. Engineers have studied the beetles' shells to design surfaces that harvest water from fog in dry regions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the beetles tilt their backs into the wind most likely to","choices":["avoid being seen by predators.","dig into the sand.","catch more fog droplets on their shells.","stay warm at night."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Droplets collect on bumps facing the fog. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.2ffe5912bc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-238","passage":"<p>For centuries, farmers in some mountain regions have raised a breed of sheep with unusually thick wool that allows the animals to survive harsh winters. As many farmers switched to faster-growing breeds, the traditional sheep became rare. Recently, some weavers have begun paying premium prices for its wool, prized for its warmth and durability, giving farmers a reason to keep raising the breed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, farmers had switched to faster-growing breeds most likely because","choices":["those breeds could be raised more profitably.","the traditional sheep could not survive winters.","faster-growing breeds produced warmer wool.","weavers refused to buy traditional wool."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Faster growth offers economic advantage. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.9bb813bc31","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-239","passage":"<p>When a city began tracking which streetlights were broken using reports from residents' smartphones, repair times dropped from weeks to days. Previously, broken lights were often discovered only during periodic inspections. The reports also revealed that some neighborhoods had many more broken lights than others, prompting the city to investigate the cause.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, before resident reports, broken lights most likely","choices":["were repaired within hours.","did not exist.","were reported by the lights themselves.","stayed broken for longer because they were found only during inspections."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They were \"discovered only during periodic inspections.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c89ab0be6a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-244","passage":"<p>When a coastal town prohibited single-use plastic bags, many shoppers switched to reusable bags. Litter surveys found far fewer plastic bags on beaches afterward. Sales of small plastic trash bags, however, increased, as some residents had previously reused shopping bags to line household wastebaskets.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, before the ban, some residents' shopping bags most likely","choices":["were always thrown directly on beaches.","were made of cloth.","were never reused.","served a second use as trash can liners."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They \"reused shopping bags to line household wastebaskets.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.ef30a2ba36","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-247","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My father's workshop still smells of pine, / though he has not lifted a plane in years. / His tools hang in their outlines on the wall, / each shape drawn in pencil, / so that anything taken / would leave a ghost behind / until it came back home.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the outlines on the wall most likely suggest that the father was","choices":["a professional painter.","careless with his tools.","unfamiliar with woodworking.","careful and organized in his work."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Each tool has a drawn place. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.df4ce596cf","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-248","passage":"<p>Researchers analyzing thousands of hours of basketball games found that teams attempting more three-point shots scored more points per possession on average, even though three-pointers are made less often than shorter shots. Because each successful three-pointer earns an extra point, the higher reward can outweigh the lower success rate. The finding helped explain why many teams increased their three-point attempts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, if three-pointers were worth the same as shorter shots, teams would most likely","choices":["score more points per possession.","attempt even more three-pointers.","attempt fewer three-pointers.","stop taking any shots."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The extra point is what makes them worthwhile. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.240bb4b283","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data11-250","passage":"<p>Geologists studying a river canyon noticed that some of the rock layers exposed in its walls contain fossils of marine animals, even though the canyon lies far inland and high above sea level. The fossils indicate that the region was once covered by a shallow sea before geological forces lifted the land.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, which statement about the region is best supported?","choices":["No animals ever lived there.","It has always been above sea level.","Its land surface has risen substantially over geological time.","It is currently covered by ocean."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The land was \"lifted.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.aaf0200ee3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-257","passage":"<p>When a regional airline started posting its on-time performance for each route on its website, some routes with poor records improved within months. Managers reported that public comparison motivated staff to find solutions, such as adjusting schedules that had been unrealistic and positioning spare parts at busy airports.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, some routes had poor on-time records partly because","choices":["the airline had too many spare parts.","the website was difficult to use.","passengers boarded too early.","their schedules had been unrealistic."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Staff adjusted \"schedules that had been unrealistic.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.59bd0cdca1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-258","passage":"<p>Some species of fireflies synchronize their flashing, so that thousands of insects in a forest blink at nearly the same moment. Researchers suggest that synchronized flashing may help females recognize males of their own species amid the visual clutter of many flashing insects. Experiments with artificial lights showed that females responded more often to synchronized flashes than to random ones.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, in a forest with many species of flashing insects, synchronized flashing would most likely","choices":["make a species' signal easier for females to pick out.","prevent all insects from flashing.","make males harder for females to find.","cause females to flash randomly."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It helps recognition \"amid the visual clutter.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.3e8826b03c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-259","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandmother spoke four languages but never learned to drive. So every Saturday, I drove her to the market across town, and every Saturday she used a different language: Portuguese with the fish seller, French with the baker, Italian with the woman who sold olives. In the car on the way home, she taught me one new word from each conversation.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the grandmother's use of different languages at the market suggests that","choices":["the market required visitors to speak French.","she wanted to confuse the narrator.","she had forgotten how to speak Portuguese.","the vendors came from varied linguistic backgrounds."],"answer":"D","explanation":"She matched each vendor's language. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.1a45bebe84","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-260","passage":"<p>A study tracking two groups of patients recovering from knee surgery found that those who began gentle physical therapy within a day of surgery regained mobility faster than those who waited a week. Patients who started early also reported less stiffness. The researchers cautioned that the early therapy must be carefully supervised to avoid injury.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers would most likely advise against","choices":["reducing stiffness.","beginning intense, unsupervised exercise right after surgery.","starting therapy within a day under supervision.","tracking patients' mobility."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Supervision is needed \"to avoid injury.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.00fefabca3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-261","passage":"<p>When archaeologists excavated an ancient harbor, they found remains of ships loaded with pottery made in regions hundreds of kilometers away. Some pottery contained traces of olive oil and wine. The discoveries suggest that the harbor was part of a trade network that connected distant communities long before historians had assumed such networks existed in the region.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, historians before these discoveries most likely believed that","choices":["the harbor was the largest in the world.","long-distance trade networks in the region developed later.","pottery was never used to store liquids.","ships could not carry cargo."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The network existed \"long before historians had assumed.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.407d159253","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-263","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The kite pulls at my hand / as if it knows the sky / belongs to it, and I / am only holding the string. / I could let go. / It would climb and climb, / and I would lose it, / and I think, some days, / that would be enough.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the speaker's thought that losing the kite \"would be enough\" most likely suggests","choices":["an acceptance that some things are meant to be free.","regret about buying the kite.","a plan to buy a larger kite.","anger at the wind."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The kite \"belongs\" to the sky. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d68fd7f225","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-264","passage":"<p>Many cities have begun converting parking spaces into small outdoor seating areas called parklets. In one city, businesses next to parklets reported increased foot traffic and sales, though some drivers complained about losing parking. A survey found that most customers of those businesses arrived on foot, by bike, or by transit rather than by car.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, business owners who initially worried that parklets would reduce sales most likely","choices":["closed their businesses.","overestimated how many of their customers depended on parking.","were correct that sales would fall.","had no customers who walked."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Most customers arrived without cars. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.5c8907e211","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-267","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>On the last day of camp, Theo's counselor handed him the old compass they had used on every hike. Theo protested that it belonged to the camp. The counselor shook his head. \"It belongs to whoever pays attention to where they're going,\" he said. Theo turned the compass over in his hands, noticing for the first time a list of initials scratched into the back.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the initials on the compass most likely belong to","choices":["the company that made the compass.","Theo's family members.","earlier campers who received the compass.","the camp's directors only."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A list of initials suggests prior recipients. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.fda8d658b4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-268","passage":"<p>When a regional water utility sent customers monthly reports comparing their water use with that of similar neighboring households, average water use fell by several percent. Households that had used more than their neighbors reduced their use the most. A few households using less than their neighbors, however, slightly increased their use after seeing the comparison.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, low-use households may have increased their water use because the reports","choices":["contained errors.","required them to use more water.","raised their water prices.","suggested they were using less than typical, making higher use seem acceptable."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Comparison to neighbors may have normalized higher use. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.29a7615e42","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-269","passage":"<p>Researchers studying ancient wheat found that farmers thousands of years ago gradually selected plants whose seed heads did not shatter and scatter seeds on the ground. Wild wheat drops its seeds when ripe, which helps it spread, but that trait made harvesting difficult. By saving seeds from plants that held onto their grain, early farmers changed the crop over generations.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a wild wheat plant that did not scatter its seeds would most likely","choices":["produce no seeds.","be impossible to harvest.","spread faster than other wild wheat.","spread less effectively on its own than typical wild wheat."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Scattering \"helps it spread.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.85dc3ade03","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data12-270","passage":"<p>A study of children's playgrounds found that equipment with some element of manageable risk, such as higher climbing structures, kept children engaged longer than very low, simple equipment. Injury rates were similar between the two types of playgrounds. Researchers suggest that appropriate challenges help children build confidence and physical skills.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the researchers would most likely recommend that playgrounds","choices":["use only flat, simple equipment.","include some age-appropriate challenges.","ban climbing.","remove all equipment above ground level."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Appropriate challenges build confidence and skills. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f3ff4090dc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-272","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Grandfather's toolbox had sat in the garage untouched since his funeral. When the porch step cracked, Esteban's mother reached for her phone to call a carpenter. Esteban stopped her. He opened the toolbox, found the hammer with the tape-wrapped handle, and felt his fingers settle into the worn places where his grandfather's had been.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, Esteban's decision to use the toolbox most likely reflects","choices":["a lack of money to hire a carpenter.","a desire to connect with his grandfather's memory.","his dislike of carpenters.","his mother's request that he fix the step."],"answer":"B","explanation":"He feels his fingers settle into his grandfather's grip. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.22d4d97262","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-274","passage":"<p>When a university library began staying open twenty-four hours during exam weeks, administrators expected crowded nights. Usage between midnight and six a.m. was lower than expected, but those who came reported that the quiet space helped them focus. The library kept the extended hours but reduced staffing during the least busy periods.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the library reduced staffing during certain hours most likely because","choices":["few people used the library at those times.","students asked for fewer staff.","the library planned to close permanently.","staff preferred working at night."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Usage was lowest during those periods. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.9229e1b4e1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-275","passage":"<p>Scientists studying the sounds of healthy forests found that dawn choruses of birds were louder and more varied in forests with greater biodiversity. In logged or degraded forests, recordings contained fewer species and longer silences. The researchers suggest that automated audio recorders could help monitor forest health across large, remote areas.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, a recording from a remote forest with long silences and few species would most likely indicate","choices":["a forest with reduced biodiversity.","an unusually healthy forest.","a problem with the recorder only.","a forest with more birds than usual."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Degraded forests had fewer species and longer silences. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.8b4d68ea9e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-277","passage":"<p>A company that manufactures phone cases switched from plastic packaging to paper made from recycled cardboard. Surveys showed that customers noticed the change and viewed the brand more favorably. Shipping costs also fell slightly because the new packaging was lighter.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the company would most likely regard the packaging change as","choices":["costly and unpopular.","irrelevant to customers.","harmful to shipping.","beneficial both for its reputation and its expenses."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Favorable views and lower costs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.58ec408afb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-278","passage":"<p>Linguists studying a remote village found that elderly residents used many words for types of snow and ice that younger residents did not know. As the climate warmed and people traveled less over frozen land, the precise terms were used less often. The researchers are recording elders' speech to preserve this detailed knowledge.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the precise terms were most likely important in the past because they","choices":["were required by village law.","helped people travel safely and knowledgeably over frozen land.","were borrowed from other languages.","described only summer weather."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Usage declined as travel over frozen land decreased. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.14cb74344f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-281","passage":"<p>Researchers measuring air quality inside schools found that classrooms with poor ventilation had high levels of carbon dioxide by midday. Students in those rooms scored lower on tests of attention given in the afternoon. When ventilation was improved, carbon dioxide levels fell and afternoon test scores rose.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, students' attention was likely weakest","choices":["in the morning in well-ventilated rooms.","in the afternoon in poorly ventilated rooms.","after ventilation was improved.","before carbon dioxide levels rose."],"answer":"B","explanation":"CO2 peaked by midday; afternoon scores were lower. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.f4c383485a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-286","passage":"<p>Farmers in one region began releasing ladybugs into greenhouses to control aphids instead of spraying insecticides. Aphid numbers fell, and the farmers saved money on chemicals. However, ladybugs sometimes flew away when food was scarce, so farmers learned to release them in smaller groups more frequently.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, releasing ladybugs in smaller groups more frequently most likely","choices":["kept enough ladybugs present without leaving them short of food.","required more insecticide.","reduced the farmers' savings.","attracted more aphids."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Scarcity caused ladybugs to leave. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.33a88bf4fc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-290","passage":"<p>When a regional power grid began paying households with rooftop solar panels and home batteries to share stored electricity during peak demand, the grid needed fewer backup power plants on hot afternoons. Households earned money, and the grid avoided building new capacity. Some households, however, worried that their batteries would wear out faster.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the grid most likely benefited because shared household power","choices":["replaced all power plants permanently.","increased electricity prices.","was used only at night.","helped meet demand during the hottest periods."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fewer backup plants were needed on hot afternoons. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.d74307c2dc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-293","passage":"<p>When a publishing company began releasing audiobooks narrated by the authors themselves, sales of those audiobooks rose compared with versions read by professional narrators. Listeners reported that hearing authors' own voices made the books feel more personal. Some reviewers noted, however, that not all authors were skilled readers.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, listeners valued author narration mainly because it","choices":["was shorter than other versions.","was always more polished.","cost less.","created a sense of personal connection to the book."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It made books \"feel more personal.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.71f0160a6e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-294","passage":"<p>Scientists examining ancient teeth found that people in one farming village had more cavities than hunter-gatherers who lived nearby at the same time. The farmers ate diets rich in grains, which break down into sugars that feed bacteria in the mouth. The finding suggests that changes in diet during the transition to farming affected dental health.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, hunter-gatherers most likely had fewer cavities because they","choices":["brushed their teeth more often.","ate only sweet fruits.","ate fewer grain-based foods.","lived in colder climates."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The farmers' grain-rich diets fed bacteria. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.e64fc344fd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-295","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The fog rolled in every afternoon at four, as reliably as a train. Newcomers complained that it ruined their plans. The fishermen, though, set their clocks by it, pulling in their nets as the gray wall crept over the harbor. \"The fog isn't the problem,\" old Marisol liked to say. \"Expecting the sun is.\"</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the fishermen differ from the newcomers mainly in that the fishermen","choices":["have never seen fog before.","adapt their routines to the fog.","leave the harbor when fog arrives.","prefer sunny weather."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They \"set their clocks by it.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.c4be50b688","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-297","passage":"<p>When a small bookstore installed a shelf labeled \"Staff Picks\" with handwritten notes explaining why employees loved each book, sales of those titles rose sharply. Customers said the notes felt like recommendations from a friend. Several books that had been sitting unsold for months became bestsellers at the store.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the notes were effective most likely because they","choices":["listed authors' awards.","made the recommendations feel personal and trustworthy.","were printed by the publisher.","lowered book prices."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They \"felt like recommendations from a friend.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.2.abbd8e568e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_IN","group":"psg-passage_data13-298","passage":"<p>Biologists studying salmon in a river with a newly removed dam found that fish began swimming upstream to spawning grounds they had not reached in decades. Within a few years, populations of insects, birds, and bears along the upper river also increased, as salmon carcasses and eggs provided food.</p>","prompt":"Based on the text, the dam had most likely","choices":["had no effect on the river.","increased food for bears.","blocked salmon from reaching upstream spawning grounds.","helped salmon reproduce."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Salmon reached areas \"they had not reached in decades.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."}
]);
